{-
   Handing a value with a teardown on out of storage that is still going to be read.

   `b = a` compiled silently and answered *three* drops for two objects. `checkTransfer` was already
   the place that noticed a silent handover, but it looked only at a *projected* load - the reading
   being that transferFrom caught the rest, since it stops at "whichever slot the value is the whole
   contents of". It does not: backingLocal matches a slot whose defining value *is* the operand,
   which is true of a call result and false of a load. So the unprojected case fell between them.

   **What is refused here is now narrower than it was**, and the difference is Design-Test.md §11.1's
   P3: a read of a whole owned local in a sink position is a *move* where the local is dead
   afterwards, so `b = a` with nothing reading `a` again is the move-assign it looks like and is
   accepted. `resolve/MoveLastUse.yana` is that half. What is left here is the case where the name
   is read again below, which is the one that would genuinely have run two teardowns - and the
   diagnostic names both lines, because a rule that depends on liveness is a rule whose second half
   is otherwise invisible.

   The advice names `copy()`. It deliberately did not until the blanket `instance (TrivialCopy(a))
   Copy(a)` and the instances beside it made `Copy` something a reader can expect to find.

   The borrowed source is the same double free reached without a load at all: an aggregate parameter
   travels as its `Arg`, so `out = v` is `assign %out, %v` with no place for the check above to look
   at. Storing it makes the caller and the destination both run its teardown, so a parameter that is
   handed on has to be taken with `->`. Passing one *as an argument* is untouched - that re-borrows,
   and an argument is not a departure point. Liveness has nothing to say about it: the caller owns
   the storage whether or not this body reads it again.

   `fn identity(v: a) -> a = v` is the same statement in return position: the signature promises the
   caller an `a` it may drop, out of storage the caller lent. The two honest spellings are `->v`,
   which takes it, and a `return` marker with a borrow result, which promises only a view.
-}

data Held {n: Int}

instance Drop(Held):
    fn drop(->self: Held) -> {}:
        n = n + self.n

let &n = 0 :: Int

-- Read again after the assignment, so the assignment is a copy and both names run the teardown.
fn assigned() -> Int:
    let &a = Held {n: 1}
    let &b = Held {n: 2}

    b = a
    return a.n

-- The same into a field, and read again through the source rather than through the destination.
fn intoField() -> Int:
    let &a = Held {n: 1}
    let &pair = Pair {left: Held {n: 2}, right: 0}

    pair.left = a
    return a.n

-- And a constructor payload, which is the shape §11.1's example has. The later use is what makes it
-- a copy; without it this is the move `MoveLastUse.yana` asserts.
fn intoLiteral() -> Int:
    let &a = Held {n: 1}
    let held = Just(a)

    let inner = match held:
        Just(h) -> h.n
        Nothing -> 0

    return a.n + inner

data Pair {left: Held, right: Int}

-- The borrowed source, written into the caller's storage.
fn intoOutParam(v: Held, &out: Held) -> {}:
    out = v

-- The same, into a constructor payload rather than an existing place.
fn intoPayload(v: Held) -> Maybe(Held) = Just(v)

-- And in return position, which is the shape `identity` has.
fn handedBack(v: Held) -> Held = v

-- Passing a borrowed value *on* as an argument stays legal: it re-borrows rather than storing, and
-- an argument is not one of the points ownership departs at.
fn reads(v: Held) -> Int = v.n

fn forwards(v: Held) -> Int = reads(v)
